{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794529127880,"sessionID":"ses_e2dbc38ceffehjLznJD2sftp6i","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2456be8001yFHeR331z8MVmp","sessionID":"ses_e2dbc38ceffehjLznJD2sftp6i","messageID":"msg_1d24565bd001aj8DRRuGZU18NK","type":"tool","id":"call_530bbb0382f2497e893eafa1","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md","offset":14,"limit":42},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md, lines 14-55\n14: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n15: \n16: `command-bridges/` is the source boundary for command metadata that is not yet represented uniformly by every scoring surface. The deriver combines live skill metadata with the authored compatibility inputs and writes the generated JSON projection.\n17: \n18: The doctor commands are a third source: the deriver reads `.skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml` and writes one bridge per doctor route and per standalone doctor command and per MCP sub-route.\n19: \n20: Each hub's `mode-registry.json` is a fourth source: the deriver reads the `command` of every mode and writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` table the advisor uses to attach a slash command to a hub recommendation.\n21: \n22: ---\n23: \n24: ## 2. CONTENTS\n25: \n26: | File | Responsibility | Authority |\n27: |---|---|---|\n28: | `command-bridges.generated.json` | Stores the derived command-bridge projection. | Generated, do not edit |\n29: | `derive-command-bridges.cjs` | Reads live metadata and authored inputs, validates uniqueness and writes the projection. | Authored deriver |\n30: | `scoring-compatibility.json` | Holds TypeScript and Python scoring compatibility mappings. | Authored input |\n31: | `shadow-diff.md` | Records comparison evidence between generated and live bridge authorities. | Authored evidence |\n32: \n33: ---\n34: \n35: ## 3. DERIVATION FLOW\n36: \n37: ```text\n38: skill command metadata files\n39: scoring-compatibility.json\n40: .skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml\n41: hub mode-registry.json files\n42:                          |\n43:                          v\n44:               derive-command-bridges.cjs\n45:                          |\n46:                          v\n47:               command-bridges.generated.json\n48: ```\n49: \n50: The deriver also updates generated projection blocks in the live TypeScript and Python scoring sources. Its `GENERATED COMMAND ROWS` block in `lib/command-attach.ts` holds `COMMAND_ROWS`, one row per id a scorer can return for an enabled entry, covering the entry id and every enabled runtime id. Each row carries the runnable slash command, its derived policy and the owning skill. The same block holds `COMMAND_POLICIES`, one policy per runnable string, so a surface that attaches a command can name its policy without a second lookup. It writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` block into the same file, one row per hub mode that names a command, and fails when a mode names a command with no file under `.skilled/commands/`. Each TypeScript bridge in `lib/scorer/projection.ts` carries an `ownerSkill`, the skill that owns its command. The generated JSON and generated source blocks are outputs, not hand-edited inputs.\n51: \n52: Each bridge row with TypeScript enabled also carries an `embeddingText`, the plain sentence a scoring vector is built from. A Python-only row carries none. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds every `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrase, because both are written in the asker's voice. The entry `description` stands in for the job only when the record states no job. A doctor row builds it from the router frontmatter `description` and the `trigger_phrases` of the route. The deriver drops each slash command string before the parts are joined, because a name left in would hand the scorer a second copy of the token it already matches literally. The field is derived on every run and never authored, so edit the sources rather than the JSON.\n53: \n54: ---\n55: \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 56]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md, lines 14-55\n14: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n15: \n16: `command-bridges/` is the source boundary for command metadata that is not yet represented uniformly by every scoring surface. The deriver combines live skill metadata with the authored compatibility inputs and writes the generated JSON projection.\n17: \n18: The doctor commands are a third source: the deriver reads `.skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml` and writes one bridge per doctor route and per standalone doctor command and per MCP sub-route.\n19: \n20: Each hub's `mode-registry.json` is a fourth source: the deriver reads the `command` of every mode and writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` table the advisor uses to attach a slash command to a hub recommendation.\n21: \n22: ---\n23: \n24: ## 2. CONTENTS\n25: \n26: | File | Responsibility | Authority |\n27: |---|---|---|\n28: | `command-bridges.generated.json` | Stores the derived command-bridge projection. | Generated, do not edit |\n29: | `derive-command-bridges.cjs` | Reads live metadata and authored inputs, validates uniqueness and writes the projection. | Authored deriver |\n30: | `scoring-compatibility.json` | Holds TypeScript and Python scoring compatibility mappings. | Authored input |\n31: | `shadow-diff.md` | Records comparison evidence between generated and live bridge authorities. | Authored evidence |\n32: \n33: ---\n34: \n35: ## 3. DERIVATION FLOW\n36: \n37: ```text\n38: skill command metadata files\n39: scoring-compatibility.json\n40: .skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml\n41: hub mode-registry.json files\n42:                          |\n43:                          v\n44:               derive-command-bridges.cjs\n45:                          |\n46:                          v\n47:               command-bridges.generated.json\n48: ```\n49: \n50: The deriver also updates generated projection blocks in the live TypeScript and Python scoring sources. Its `GENERATED COMMAND ROWS` block in `lib/command-attach.ts` holds `COMMAND_ROWS`, one row per id a scorer can return for an enabled entry, covering the entry id and every enabled runtime id. Each row carries the runnable slash command, its derived policy and the owning skill. The same block holds `COMMAND_POLICIES`, one policy per runnable string, so a surface that attaches a command can name its policy without a second lookup. It writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` block into the same file, one row per hub mode that names a command, and fails when a mode names a command with no file under `.skilled/commands/`. Each TypeScript bridge in `lib/scorer/projection.ts` carries an `ownerSkill`, the skill that owns its command. The generated JSON and generated source blocks are outputs, not hand-edited inputs.\n51: \n52: Each bridge row with TypeScript enabled also carries an `embeddingText`, the plain sentence a scoring vector is built from. A Python-only row carries none. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds every `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrase, because both are written in the asker's voice. The entry `description` stands in for the job only when the record states no job. A doctor row builds it from the router frontmatter `description` and the `trigger_phrases` of the route. The deriver drops each slash command string before the parts are joined, because a name left in would hand the scorer a second copy of the token it already matches literally. The field is derived on every run and never authored, so edit the sources rather than the JSON.\n53: \n54: ---\n55: \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 56]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md\",\"offset\":14,\"limit\":42}"},"time":{"start":1794529127400,"end":1794529127880}}}}
